PREAMBLE

This license governs works of any digital kind — including but not limited to
software, source code, designs, visual assets, documents, datasets, and models
(each, the "Work"). It is written to be as short and permissive as possible
while replacing the traditional notion of a fixed original author with a
traceable chain of contributing groups.

Genuine authorship is increasingly difficult to establish: a work can be
reproduced quickly and convincingly by many hands. This license therefore does
not privilege a single original owner. It instead recognizes the groups that
take responsibility for a Work over time. A contribution carries weight only
when a structured group stands behind it; a machine cannot stand behind
anything, and so artificial intelligence holds no independent standing under
this license. Where AI is used, the responsibility for the result rests with
the group that verifies it, not with the tool.

GRANT

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person or group obtaining
a copy of this Work, to deal in the Work without restriction, including without
limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute,
sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Work, and to permit persons to whom the
Work is furnished to do so, including within closed-source and commercial
products, subject to the conditions below.

CONDITIONS

1. Credit is kept at the level of a Group, never of an individual. A "Group" is
   any structured collective — an organization, company, team, or other body —
   that has a declared structure and a clearly identifiable top responsible
   party, and that remains identifiable and reachable. An individual may
   satisfy this by forming their own Group, even one consisting solely of its
   owner.

2. When a Work carries credits, those credits must be carried forward. This may
   be done in either of two ways, at the deriving Group's discretion:
     (a) by listing the upstream Groups, in whole or in part; or
     (b) by crediting the upstream Work itself, which is sufficient to
         represent the entire chain of Groups behind it.
   A reference under (b) should identify the upstream Work by name and its
   responsible Group, so that it remains traceable even if a link becomes
   unavailable.

3. Credits need not be displayed in the interface of any product, but must
   remain findable by a party who looks for them.

4. A credit obligation persists only while a substantial portion of the
   upstream Work is still used and still relevant. Once that is no longer the
   case, whether and how long to retain the credit is left to the judgment and
   policy of the deriving Group. No rule can anticipate every situation; that
   discretion is where a Group's own policy applies.

5. This Work may be used to train artificial-intelligence models. In that case
   the credits need not appear in the model or its output, but the origin of
   the Work must remain clear and traceable — for example, recorded in the
   dataset documentation or the model's accompanying materials.

